]. The candidate will be the primary researcher for the ethnographic fieldwork component of a multi-year study examining how rural Michigan communities have adapted to decades of workplace automation in manufacturing and agriculture. Working across case sites, the postdoc will conduct sustained observational fieldwork and interviews with current and former workers, business owners and managers, families, and community leaders, documenting how the impact of automation in the workplace unfolds and how communities respond. The postdoc will be embedded in each community for extended periods, working alongside the PI, Co-PI, and a team of graduate student researchers to capture both workplace-level dynamics and broader community-level adaptation.

Applicants must have their PhD (or be ABD) in a field related to the project, including but not limited to: information science, anthropology, sociology, HCI, communication, science and technology studies, etc. You will be expected to have PhD in hand at time of appointment. Applicants must have substantial experience in ethnographic and qualitative fieldwork methods, including in-depth interviewing and participant observation. Applicants must also have a driver’s license and reliable form of transportation as extensive field-based travel is required for the position. The position is located in Michigan and remote work is not possible. We are going to start reviewing applications in September with the hope that the postdoc would start by early next year. Though, for a very strong candidate, the start timeline could be flexible.

